The client is expected to check whether  p is a safe 2048-bit prime (meaning that both p and (p-1)/2 are prime, and that
2^2047<<2^2048), and that g generates a cyclic subgroup of prime order (p-1)/2, i.e. is a quadratic residue mod p.
Since g is always equal to 2,3,4,5,6 or 7 this is easily done using quadratic reciprocity law, yielding a simple condition
on p mod 4g -- namely, p mod 8 =7 for g=2; p mod3=2 for g=3l no extra condition for g=4; p mod 5 =1 or 4 for g = 5; p mod 24 = 19 or
23 for g = 6; and p mod 7 = 3, 5 or 6 for g = 7. After g and p have been chechked by the client, it  makes sense to cache
the result, so as to avoid repeating lengthy computations in future. This cache might be shared with one used for
